# Manuscript PDF page 227

                              THE CHORD



   My hand would not open. It had closed on the coin and stayed
closed, and the metal was burning: a ribbon of smoke curled from the
cloth under it, and I saw my own skin pressed to the copper and knew
I had gone on holding long after the work stopped needing me.
   Mikey took my wrist.
   He didn’t pull. He turned my hand over, gently, so the back of it
faced him, and with two knuckles he rapped on it, twice, flat and plain,
on the bone.
   Dos.
   I’m here.
   My fingers opened.
   I’d like to tell you I decided to. My hand simply knew that knock the
way it knew the landing and the stairwell and the wall between 4C and
4D, and it opened, like a hand that has finally been told it may put
something down.
   The coin dropped into Mikey’s open palm, hot enough to hiss, and
he didn’t flinch.
   He closed his fist on it until it stopped hissing. Then he put it in my
shirt pocket and buttoned the pocket, the way my mother used to do
with lunch money.
   I fell.
   Yanaisa and Mikey eased me down clumsily, keeping my skull off
the carpet. Manny dropped his drum and swore at his ribs. Overhead
the steel roof stayed exactly where a hundred years of labor had set it.
   I could see everyone’s mouths.
   On the right there was no world at all.



What I remember is cold carpet through my shirt and Yari on her knees
in my line of sight, two fingers already on my pulse, reciting for a med‐
ic I hadn’t seen arrive: “Sudden loss, right ear, onset ten-seventeen.”
Somebody said steroids, there’s a window. My mother’s name lit a
phone, and then the phone swung away, because a stranger was pick‐
ing it up and not asking me to run the conversation.




